WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.640
Hi friends!

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.013
Have you ever wondered how a little stool can hold you up?

00:00:04.013 --> 00:00:04.885
Let's find out!

00:00:05.885 --> 00:00:08.144
The Earth is always pulling you down.

00:00:08.144 --> 00:00:09.852
That pull is called gravity.

00:00:09.852 --> 00:00:12.904
When you sit, your weight pushes down on the seat.

00:00:13.904 --> 00:00:16.951
The seat spreads your push out into all the legs.

00:00:16.951 --> 00:00:19.624
Each leg carries a share of the load, so no

00:00:19.624 --> 00:00:21.179
one leg has to do it all.

00:00:22.179 --> 00:00:24.259
The legs push down on the floor.

00:00:24.259 --> 00:00:27.055
And the floor pushes back up, just as hard!

00:00:27.055 --> 00:00:28.291
Push down, push up.

00:00:28.291 --> 00:00:30.307
They balance, so nothing moves.

00:00:31.307 --> 00:00:32.782
Why three or four legs?

00:00:32.782 --> 00:00:34.899
Spread out feet make a wide base.

00:00:34.899 --> 00:00:38.298
Your balance point, right in the middle of you, stays

00:00:38.298 --> 00:00:39.324
inside the feet.

00:00:39.324 --> 00:00:40.992
So the stool stays steady.

00:00:41.992 --> 00:00:45.095
But if you lean too far, your balance point goes

00:00:45.095 --> 00:00:46.194
outside the feet.

00:00:46.194 --> 00:00:47.035
Then, whoops!

00:00:47.035 --> 00:00:48.328
The stool tips over.

00:00:49.328 --> 00:00:51.993
So sit in the middle, keep your feet down, and

00:00:51.993 --> 00:00:54.021
don't rock or lean back on a stool.

00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:58.397
Stools are made of strong stuff, like wood or metal.

00:00:58.397 --> 00:01:01.967
Straight legs are great at being squished from the top.

00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:03.719
That is called compression.

00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:05.667
A straight leg can hold a lot!

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:09.844
These bars between the legs are called cross braces.

00:01:09.844 --> 00:01:13.143
They stop the legs from spreading out sideways, so the

00:01:13.143 --> 00:01:14.304
stool stays strong.

00:01:15.304 --> 00:01:15.873
Try this!

00:01:15.873 --> 00:01:19.097
Stand four paper cups upside down, and gently put a

00:01:19.097 --> 00:01:19.856
book on top.

00:01:19.856 --> 00:01:22.511
The cups share the weight, and hold it up!

00:01:22.511 --> 00:01:23.965
Ask a grown-up to help.

00:01:24.965 --> 00:01:28.114
Gravity pulls down, the legs share the push, and the

00:01:28.114 --> 00:01:29.385
floor pushes back up.

00:01:29.385 --> 00:01:31.323
That's how a stool holds you up!
